WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.033 --> 00:00:04.913
A longer neurite grows faster.

2
00:00:05.793 --> 00:00:07.193
Is that enough to make one winner?

3
00:00:07.833 --> 00:00:09.713
Here is a model, not a measurement.

4
00:00:10.517 --> 00:00:11.997
Start with five neurites.

5
00:00:12.897 --> 00:00:17.357
Four are 15 micrometres long, and one is 20, 5 micrometres ahead.

6
00:00:17.967 --> 00:00:20.807
Each neurite grows in proportion to its own length.

7
00:00:21.547 --> 00:00:22.987
A longer one grows faster.

8
00:00:23.617 --> 00:00:25.177
Run the rule for two days.

9
00:00:25.177 --> 00:00:27.017
Does one neurite win?

10
00:00:32.567 --> 00:00:35.027
No. All five grow.

11
00:00:35.967 --> 00:00:39.747
The leader stays the same fraction ahead, a third longer than each sibling.

12
00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:44.310
The gap in micrometres widens, but nothing holds the others back.

13
00:00:44.883 --> 00:00:47.943
You end with five long neurites, and no single winner.

14
00:00:48.567 --> 00:00:50.747
Growing faster the more it grows is true.

15
00:00:51.307 --> 00:00:54.647
But growth in simple proportion to length picks no winner.

16
00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:57.670
The leader's gain must come at the others' cost.

17
00:00:58.570 --> 00:00:59.770
Two ingredients do this.

18
00:01:00.283 --> 00:01:02.743
First, positive feedback within a neurite.

19
00:01:03.883 --> 00:01:05.703
Growth recruits more of whatever drives growth.

20
00:01:06.583 --> 00:01:08.863
Second, competition between neurites.

21
00:01:09.643 --> 00:01:13.303
One proposal: they draw on a limited supply from the cell body.

22
00:01:13.917 --> 00:01:16.297
So one neurite's gain is the others' loss.

23
00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:19.390
But even a shared supply is not quite enough.

24
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.600
If each neurite takes a share in proportion to its length, the fractions again stay

25
00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:25.583
fixed.

26
00:01:25.583 --> 00:01:30.283
A long neurite's growth, relative to its own length, has to be larger than a short

27
00:01:30.283 --> 00:01:32.343
neurite's. There are two ways.

28
00:01:33.033 --> 00:01:35.193
The feedback could be steeper than proportional.

29
00:01:36.133 --> 00:01:40.413
Or every neurite could pay the same fixed running cost, such as retraction.

30
00:01:41.033 --> 00:01:44.253
That cost takes a bigger bite out of a short neurite's growth.

31
00:01:44.867 --> 00:01:48.027
With either one, a small lead grows into a large one.

32
00:01:48.867 --> 00:01:51.627
The others grow about fourfold, then slow to crawl.

33
00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:55.020
With both, the others shrink back to stubs.

34
00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:56.560
Now there is one winner.

35
00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:58.970
Now place the molecules in the loop.

36
00:01:59.533 --> 00:02:02.733
PI3-kinase makes the lipid PIP3.

37
00:02:03.513 --> 00:02:05.673
It builds up at the tip of the future axon.

38
00:02:06.817 --> 00:02:09.357
PIP3 is a mark at one spot on the membrane.

39
00:02:10.237 --> 00:02:13.057
It draws proteins with PH domains to that spot.

40
00:02:13.667 --> 00:02:18.067
The Par3/Par6/aPKC complex, which also marks the top of an epithelial cell, gathers

41
00:02:18.067 --> 00:02:19.067
there.

42
00:02:19.167 --> 00:02:23.587
At the tip, the switches Cdc42 and Rac reorganize actin.

43
00:02:24.117 --> 00:02:29.017
Loosened actin is proposed to let microtubules push forward into the growth cone.

44
00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:31.770
Part of the loop is thought to be physical.

45
00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:35.530
A faster neurite draws more material and more transport.

46
00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:37.690
Then it grows faster still.

47
00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:39.610
The loop closes.

48
00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:41.600
There is also a brake.

49
00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:43.980
RhoA tends to oppose neurite growth.

50
00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:46.820
What holds the losers back is less settled.

51
00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:50.920
A limited supply and an inhibitory signal are both proposed.

52
00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:55.370
So a winner needs competition, plus something that compounds a lead.

53
00:02:56.410 --> 00:02:57.450
Next, can we rig the race?
